SF CRIS                                                                         
OB To develop tissue culture and gene transfer techniques to improve dis        
   ease resistance and morphogenesis in peach, soybean, and tobacco and         
   to determine the role of ABA in root morphogenesis of tissue-cultured        
    plants.                                                                     
AP 1) Investigations with peach, soybean, and tobacco will focus on tran        
   sfer- ring genes via Agrobacterium-mediated gene transfer and electro        
   poration. DNA to be transferred will carry an antibiotic resistance m        
   arker to permit selection of transformed cells prior to regeneration         
   and either phytohor- mone-specifying genes calculated to overcome our        
    inability to regenerate peach and soybean plants from cell cultures         
   that confer resistance to several bacterial pathogens. 2) Investigati        
   ons with peach will also include testing the response of peach regene        
   rants in vitro and under greenhouse conditions to several peach patho        
   gens as well as evaluating the field performance of peach plants deri        
   ved from tissue cultures. Immunofluorescence, GC/MS and electron micr        
   oscopy techniques will be used to study the role of ABA in root forma        
   tion of in vitro propagated peach shoots. Beltsville, MD; BG 006, Roo        
   m 1, BL-1; RD-35; 06/10/91. Scientists: F. A. Hammerschlag and L. D.         
   Owens.                                                                       
DE GENE-TRANSFER TISSUE-CULTURE PEACHES SOYBEANS TOBACCO PLANT-IMPROVEME        
   NT TREE-IMPROVEMENT CROP-PRODUCTION PLANT-GENETICS GENETIC-ENGINEERIN        
   G PLANT-DISEASE-RESISTANCE MORPHOGENESIS ROOTS ROOT-DEVELOPMENT AGROB        
   ACTERIUM ANTIBIOTIC-RESISTANCE MARKERS                                       
PR Field studies of tissue-cultured (TC), own-rooted peach cultivars ind        
   icated thatTC trees produced significantly more fruit compared with b        
   ud-grafted trees 2 and 3 years after planting in the field. These stu        
   dies should be of interest to scientists and peach growers since they        
    demonstrate the feasibility of using TC-propagation to produce own-r        
   ooted peach trees. Through in vitro selection and screening for somac        
   lonal variants, peach plants have been obtained with stable increased        
    levels of resistance to Xanthomonas campestris pv. pruni and Pseudom        
   onas syringae pv. syringae. These studies should be of interest to ot        
   her scientists concerned with improving woody crop species. An in vit        
   ro screening system for evaluating peach germplasm for root-knot nema        
   tode resistance was developed that shortens the screening period from        
    3 years to 5 weeks. This system can be used to evaluate seedlings an        
   d peach plants derived from tissue culture and genetic engineering st        
   udies. An Agrobacterium-mediated gene transfer system for peach has b        
   een developed for peach which has been used to introduce the cytokini        
   n gene into peach and will be used as the basis for future gene trans        
   fer studies. A gene vector pGT100 was constructed for use with the su        
   pervirulent Agrobacterium tumefaciens helper strain EHA101. pGT100/EH        
   A101 proved highly efficient in transferring genes to tobacco and soy        
   bean. These studies may lead to improved methods for scientists to tr        
   ansfer genes to these difficult to transform crops.                          
